description:
When a loop contains a try statement, and break or continue fires inside the try body, the emitted jump patch position is corrupted.

Root cause: compileTryStmt (compiler.ts ~line 1177) creates separate sub-compiler instances for try/catch/finally bodies (each with their own ByteBuffer starting at position 0), but SHARES the parent's loopStack array. When break or continue fires inside the sub-compiler body, it calls emitActionJump() which returns the jump's SI16-field position relative to the sub-compiler's buf (e.g. position 5). This position is pushed into ctx.breakPatches or ctx.continuePatches. Later, the parent compiler calls patchJump(p, loopEnd) which patches this.buf (the parent's buffer) at position p — but p is a sub-buffer offset, not a parent-buffer offset. The patch corrupts whatever bytes happen to be at that position in the parent buffer, producing invalid AVM1 bytecode.

Minimal reproduction:
  for (var i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    try {
      if (i == 5) break;   // break exits the for loop, inside the try
    } catch(e) { }
  }

Expected: loop runs for i=0..4 then breaks.
Actual: break-patch is at wrong position in parent buffer → corrupted AVM1 bytecode.

Same issue exists for continue inside try/catch/finally, and break/continue in catch/finally sub-compilers.

Fix: when copying sub-compiled bytes into the parent buffer, adjust all break/continue patch positions by the sub-buffer's parent offset (parentBuf.length at the time the sub-bytes are written). Or inline try compilation without sub-compilers. See packages/core/src/as2/compiler.ts lines 1177-1265.
